---
title: Mallard
---
# Mallard

The mallard is the commonest duck of the northern hemisphere and the ancestor of
almost every domestic duck. It dabbles at the surface and upends in shallow
water, straining seeds, invertebrates and plant matter through the lamellae of
the bill rather than descending for them. The female makes a down-lined hollow on
the ground, often far from water, and leads the ducklings to it within hours of
hatching. Drakes take on a drab eclipse plumage during the flightless moult of
midsummer. See [[anatidae]].
